[brief] Note first: I had no hydrodynamics emulator or Pk-ratio tool in this session. My tools were literature search only, so I did not compute P_hydro/P_DMO at z=0 for any parameter point. That has to be do
**Survey** — 29 tools on 2 servers, 5 read-only probes, 19 papers found.

Note first: I had no hydrodynamics emulator or Pk-ratio tool in this session. My tools were literature search only, so I did not compute P_hydro/P_DMO at z=0 for any parameter point. That has to be done downstream with an emulator the team actually has, such as BCemu, BACCO, SP(k)/pyspk or the FLAMINGO GP emulator. The search_arxiv_abstracts tool returned HTTP 429 (rate-limited) on all three calls, so everything below comes from search_arxiv and covers abstracts only. The literature broadly agrees that AGN feedback is the main driver of suppression. It pushes gas out of group-scale haloes (M ~ 1e13-1e14 Msun/h), from inside r500 to beyond r200m. This lowers total matter power from about 1% at k~0.3 h/Mpc to about 10-30% near k~1-10 h/Mpc at z=0. Radiative cooling raises power only at very small scales, about k>70 h/Mpc in OWLS. Across simulations, the size of the suppression tracks the mean baryon (or gas) fraction in groups and clusters closely (van Daalen+2019, SP(k)). Predictions still differ widely between simulations: Horizon-AGN and Astrid show weak or almost no suppression at z=0, while FLAMINGO jet models, BAHAMAS and the DES Y3 weak-lensing + ACT kSZ fits show strong suppression. For weak lensing, FLAMINGO finds that realistic feedback is too weak to remove the S8 tension. Amon and Efstathiou and Preston et al. find that resolving it would need suppression stronger than most simulations give, reaching down to k~0.2 h/Mpc. The DES Y3 + kSZ joint fit does prefer such a more extreme feedback scenario, with S8=0.823+0.019-0.020.

Quantities worth checking: Pk-ratio S(k)=P_hydro/P_DMO at z=0 over k=0.1-10 h/Mpc for at least two feedback settings (e.g. weak versus strong ejection): the depth of the minimum, the k where it occurs, and the k where suppression first reaches 1%; Mean baryon fraction f_b(M500 ~ 1e14 Msun) / (Omega_b/Omega_m) for each point, checked against the SP(k) / van Daalen+2019 relation; BCemu/BACCO parameters: log Mc (gas-retention mass scale), theta_ej (ejection radius), eta, beta; how the suppression responds to Mc; Maximum suppression near k~7 h/Mpc compared with the 20-28% bound from X-ray gas fractions (Giri and Schneider); Suppression at k~0.2-1 h/Mpc compared with what the Amon and Efstathiou / Preston et al. S8 solution requires; Implied fractional change in the cosmic-shear C_ell (ell~500-5000) and the resulting shift in S8 if feedback is ignored (shift of order 1 sigma for DES/KiDS).
